// @contract: C-226, C-033
// `list.push(h.f, v)` and `list.clear(h.f)` where `h` is a record var or a
// `mut` record parameter: the builder idiom — accumulate into a list field —
// builds and runs on the wasm leg, and the caller's data is mutated through
// the `mut` parameter exactly as on native (C-226).
//
// Before #2411 the structural leg's `push` arm accepted only a plain var
// receiver and refused a field (`list-push-nonvar`); the incumbent brick
// refused the enclosing loop for its own reason, so this shape — the one #2316
// was found in, gramide's document builder — had NO wasm route at all. It now
// routes through the copy-on-write field write the leg already owns:
// `h.f = h.f + [v]`, so the record is copied, the replaced slot's credit is
// released, the fresh list takes its own credit, and the var is rebound.
// No second receiver mode; the credits ride the path `lower_field_assign`
// pins under C-033.
//
// Rows, and what each is for:
//   str/float/rec  the element's runtime shape — a heap element (String,
//               a record) must take its credit in the concat, a scalar must
//               not; Float crosses the push helper as a bit pattern
//   mut param   a `mut` record parameter's field pushed from a callee — the
//               caller's record carries the grown list (C-226)
//   alias       C-033: an alias taken BEFORE the push keeps the old list —
//               in-place mutation stays unobservable, the record was copied
//
// DECLARED OMISSIONS, three rows carried by tests/list_push_on_record_field_test.rs
// (it builds and runs both legs directly): the #2316 builder verbatim (a
// `mut` param, a `for` loop, an `if` branch, two pushes per iteration), a
// 1000-push `for` loop, and `list.clear(h.f)` followed by a push. The
// incumbent brick refuses a scalar `for`-in loop variable in an exported fn
// ("outside the MIR-lowering subset") and has NO definition of `list.clear`
// at all (an unlinked call is a wall), so any of those rows here would cost
// the two determinism wall ceilings and the walled-real baseline — three
// shrink-only ratchets — for coverage the test provides; the same trade
// C-076's `EXTENDED (#2133)` and C-334's `EXTENDED (#2398)` clauses record.
// The record row reads its element through a `let` because the incumbent
// also refuses a `??`-bearing interpolation in call-argument position. A
// nested receiver path (`h.i.xs`) is still refused (`list-push-nonvar`) and
// is pinned as a wall by the same test.
type Names = { names: List[String], n: Int }

type Floats = { xs: List[Float] }

type Ints = { xs: List[Int] }

type B = { k: Int }

type Boxes = { bs: List[B] }

fn add_name(mut h: Names, s: String) -> Unit = {
  list.push(h.names, s)
  h.n = h.n + 1
}

fn main() -> Unit = {
  var nm = Names { names: [], n: 0 }
  add_name(nm, "a")
  add_name(nm, "bb")
  println("str     ${nm.names} ${nm.n}")
  var fl = Floats { xs: [] }
  list.push(fl.xs, 1.5)
  list.push(fl.xs, 2.5)
  println("float   ${fl.xs}")
  var bx = Boxes { bs: [] }
  list.push(bx.bs, B { k: 7 })
  list.push(bx.bs, B { k: 8 })
  let second = list.get(bx.bs, 1) ?? B { k: 0 }
  println("rec     ${list.len(bx.bs)} ${second.k}")
  var al = Ints { xs: [1] }
  let before = al
  list.push(al.xs, 2)
  println("alias   ${before.xs} ${al.xs}")
}
